## Constraints from the existing codebase

- Frontend must use vanilla HTML, CSS, and JavaScript (no frameworks like React, Vue, etc.)
- Express server must listen on port **27107** specifically (non-standard port)
- Must connect to local MongoDB instance at `mongodb://127.0.0.1:27017` (no cloud services)
- Database name must be `temp_work_db` with `contacts` and `testimonials` collections
- Existing HTML/CSS/JS files from T-001 should be refined/extended, not discarded
- Frontend must remain fully functional even if backend server is not running (graceful degradation)
- Contact form error handling must display error message and allow retry (not silent fallback)
- Testimonials must be fetched from MongoDB via API, not hardcoded in HTML
- Test must be runnable with `npm test` without external services
- File structure must enable parallel editing (separate files to minimize merge conflicts)
- Contact form fields (name, email, message) already defined in HTML; validation must handle these
- Mobile-responsive layout already defined; enhancements must maintain responsive behavior
- Smooth scrolling already implemented in CSS; additional JS enhancements must complement existing behavior

## Open risks

- Port 27107 is non-standard and may conflict with other services; planner should verify this choice doesn't conflict in the user's environment
- Testimonials section requires both API endpoint and MongoDB collection to be created and wired together; if these tasks run in parallel, they must be synchronized in integration phase
- Contact form error/retry flow adds complexity to frontend JS; must handle both successful submission, API errors, and unreachable backend gracefully
- Testimonials endpoint must be tested to ensure it returns data in format expected by frontend display logic
- MongoDB `testimonials` collection requires seed data or initialization logic; unclear if this should be manual setup or automated in test/seed script

